--- DESCRIPTION ---
The three set tracks for Section B: section-by-section keys, the production facts, and the terms that earn marks.

--- CONTENT_HTML ---
<figure class="sv-annotated-player sv-ap-yt" data-lesson-id="dc05b5cb-a473-4721-9715-db1f1cf84232"><div class="sv-ap-bar"><button type="button" class="sv-ap-play">&#9654;</button><span class="sv-ap-tick">0:00 / &ndash;:&ndash;&ndash;</span><div class="sv-ap-tracks"><button type="button" class="sv-ap-trackbtn sv-ap-trackbtn--on" data-track="t1" data-yt="fJ9rUzIMcZQ" data-dur="359">Bohemian Rhapsody</button><button type="button" class="sv-ap-trackbtn" data-track="t2" data-yt="0e4Odk-v3oU" data-dur="187">Seven Seas of Rhye</button><button type="button" class="sv-ap-trackbtn" data-track="t3" data-yt="_IL77qFKgRw" data-dur="217">Love of My Life</button></div></div><div class="sv-ap-media"><div class="sv-ap-video"><div class="sv-ap-ytmount"></div></div><div class="sv-ap-wrap"><div class="sv-ap-track"><div class="sv-ap-trackfill"></div></div><button type="button" class="sv-ap-pin" data-track="t1" data-cid="t1c1" data-t="0" style="left:0.00%">1<span class="sv-ap-tip"><strong>1 &middot; Intro</strong>A cappella &mdash; voices alone, no instruments. Homorhythmic: every voice moves in the same rhythm, like one instrument.</span></button><button type="button" class="sv-ap-pin" data-track="t1" data-cid="t1c2" data-t="49" style="left:13.65%">2<span class="sv-ap-tip"><strong>2 &middot; Ballad</strong>Arpeggiated accompaniment &mdash; the piano plays each chord one note at a time, rippling under the voice.</span></button><button type="button" class="sv-ap-pin" data-track="t1" data-cid="t1c3" data-t="156" style="left:43.45%">3<span class="sv-ap-tip"><strong>3 &middot; Guitar solo</strong>Virtuosic &mdash; deliberately difficult, high-register playing that shows off the player.</span></button><button type="button" class="sv-ap-pin" data-track="t1" data-cid="t1c4" data-t="183" style="left:50.97%">4<span class="sv-ap-tip"><strong>4 &middot; Opera</strong>Abrupt modulation &mdash; the key jumps to A major, unrelated to home. Listen for question-and-answer phrases between the voices.</span></button><button type="button" class="sv-ap-pin" data-track="t1" data-cid="t1c5" data-t="247" style="left:68.80%">5<span class="sv-ap-tip"><strong>5 &middot; Hard rock</strong>12/8 compound time &mdash; each beat now splits into threes. Count four strong beats, each carrying a triplet.</span></button><button type="button" class="sv-ap-pin" data-track="t1" data-cid="t1c6" data-t="295" style="left:82.17%">6<span class="sv-ap-tip"><strong>6 &middot; Outro</strong>Ending away from home &mdash; the song closes in F major, not the key it began in.</span></button><button type="button" class="sv-ap-pin" data-track="t2" data-cid="t2c1" data-t="5" style="left:0.00%">1<span class="sv-ap-tip"><strong>1 &middot; Piano intro</strong>Cross-rhythm arpeggios &mdash; the pianist&rsquo;s right hand ripples against the left in a clashing pattern.</span></button><button type="button" class="sv-ap-pin" data-track="t2" data-cid="t2c2" data-t="25" style="left:10.16%">2<span class="sv-ap-tip"><strong>2 &middot; Verse</strong>Power chords &mdash; bare root-and-fifth guitar chords with no third: the hard rock sound.</span></button><button type="button" class="sv-ap-pin" data-track="t2" data-cid="t2c3" data-t="63" style="left:37.97%">3<span class="sv-ap-tip"><strong>3 &middot; Middle</strong>The bridge &mdash; the mode shifts and a new melody breaks the verse pattern. It ends with a long held vocal cry that launches the guitar solo.</span></button><button type="button" class="sv-ap-pin" data-track="t2" data-cid="t2c4" data-t="84" style="left:49.73%">4<span class="sv-ap-tip"><strong>4 &middot; Guitar solo</strong>Instrumental contrast &mdash; the guitar takes the melodic lead between sung verses.</span></button><button type="button" class="sv-ap-pin" data-track="t2" data-cid="t2c5" data-t="157" style="left:77.01%">5<span class="sv-ap-tip"><strong>5 &middot; Seaside outro</strong>Music-hall pastiche &mdash; the style flips genre completely for a theatrical ending.</span></button><button type="button" class="sv-ap-pin" data-track="t3" data-cid="t3c1" data-t="0" style="left:0.00%">1<span class="sv-ap-tip"><strong>1 &middot; Introduction</strong>Bars 1&ndash;7. Implies F major, then slips to B flat major in its second phrase.</span></button><button type="button" class="sv-ap-pin" data-track="t3" data-cid="t3c2" data-t="23" style="left:10.60%">2<span class="sv-ap-tip"><strong>2 &middot; Verses 1 and 2</strong>Bars 8&ndash;31. They start in C major and modulate to F major.</span></button><button type="button" class="sv-ap-pin" data-track="t3" data-cid="t3c3" data-t="97" style="left:44.70%">3<span class="sv-ap-tip"><strong>3 &middot; Instrumental bridge</strong>Bars 31&ndash;35, in B flat major &mdash; an echo of the introduction&rsquo;s second phrase.</span></button><button type="button" class="sv-ap-pin" data-track="t3" data-cid="t3c4" data-t="113" style="left:52.07%">4<span class="sv-ap-tip"><strong>4 &middot; Interlude</strong>Bars 36&ndash;44. D minor, the relative minor, passing through G minor.</span></button><button type="button" class="sv-ap-pin" data-track="t3" data-cid="t3c5" data-t="143" style="left:65.90%">5<span class="sv-ap-tip"><strong>5 &middot; Guitar solo</strong>Bars 45&ndash;58. Starts in F major, moves to C major, then B flat major.</span></button><button type="button" class="sv-ap-pin" data-track="t3" data-cid="t3c6" data-t="188" style="left:86.64%">6<span class="sv-ap-tip"><strong>6 &middot; Coda</strong>Bars 59&ndash;67. Begins in D minor and finishes in F major &mdash; home at last.</span></button></div></div><figcaption class="sv-listen-credit">Official videos, streamed from YouTube &mdash; not hosted by StudyVault. Queen: Bohemian Rhapsody (1975), Seven Seas of Rhye (1974), Love of My Life (1975).</figcaption></figure><div class="sv-listening"><section class="sv-card sv-card--cover" data-title="The three tracks"><h2>Queen: three set tracks, equal weight</h2><div class="sv-card-body"><p data-narration-id="q1">Your study pieces are three Queen tracks: <em>Bohemian Rhapsody</em>, <em>Seven Seas of Rhye</em> and <em>Love of My Life</em>. Questions can ask about any of them, so know all three equally. The band: Freddie Mercury (lead vocals, piano), Brian May (electric guitar, vocals), John Deacon (bass guitar) and Roger Taylor (drum kit, vocals).</p></div></section><section class="sv-card" data-title="The set recordings"><h2>The set recordings</h2><div class="sv-card-body"><div class="key-fact" data-narration-id="q2" data-revision-tip="Cover the box, then write from memory all three set recordings: Bohemian Rhapsody, Seven Seas of Rhye, Love of My Life."><div class="key-fact-label">Key Fact &mdash; The Set Recordings</div><p><em>Bohemian Rhapsody</em> and <em>Love of My Life</em> come from <em>A Night at the Opera</em> (1975). <em>Seven Seas of Rhye</em> comes from <em>Queen II</em> (1974). The set versions are the 2011 remastered recordings.</p></div></div></section><section class="sv-card" data-title="Six sections" data-track="t1" data-chapters="t1c1,t1c2,t1c3,t1c4,t1c5,t1c6"><h2>Bohemian Rhapsody: six sections, no chorus</h2><div class="sv-card-body"><p data-narration-id="q3">There is no repeating chorus at all &mdash; the song is <dfn class="term" data-def="Music that keeps moving forward with no repeated sections.">through-composed</dfn>, which was very unusual for a 1970s rock single. There are four contrasting sections plus a coda. It opens <dfn class="term" data-def="Voices alone, without instruments.">a cappella</dfn><button type="button" class="sv-ap-ref" data-t="0" data-track="t1">1</button>, in <dfn class="term" data-def="Every voice moving in the same rhythm at the same time.">homorhythmic</dfn> block chords. A piano ballad follows<button type="button" class="sv-ap-ref" data-t="49" data-track="t1">2</button>, with <dfn class="term" data-def="Chords played one note at a time, in a rippling pattern.">arpeggiated</dfn> accompaniment.</p><p data-narration-id="q4">Then come a <dfn class="term" data-def="Deliberately difficult, showing off the performer&rsquo;s skill.">virtuosic</dfn> guitar solo<button type="button" class="sv-ap-ref" data-t="156" data-track="t1">3</button>, an opera section<button type="button" class="sv-ap-ref" data-t="183" data-track="t1">4</button>, a hard rock section<button type="button" class="sv-ap-ref" data-t="247" data-track="t1">5</button>, and a quiet, reflective outro<button type="button" class="sv-ap-ref" data-t="295" data-track="t1">6</button>.</p></div></section><section class="sv-card" data-title="Keys and metre" data-track="t1"><h2>Bohemian Rhapsody: keys and metre</h2><div class="sv-card-body"><p data-narration-id="q5">The a cappella opening and the ballad are both in B flat major &mdash; the very first chord is B flat 6, an unusual added-sixth chord<button type="button" class="sv-ap-ref" data-t="0" data-track="t1">1</button><button type="button" class="sv-ap-ref" data-t="49" data-track="t1">2</button>. The rock section that follows the opera is in E flat major<button type="button" class="sv-ap-ref" data-t="247" data-track="t1">5</button>. The opera section jumps to A major, an unrelated key<button type="button" class="sv-ap-ref" data-t="183" data-track="t1">4</button> &mdash; that jump is a <dfn class="term" data-def="A change of key.">modulation</dfn>. The song then ends on an F major chord<button type="button" class="sv-ap-ref" data-t="295" data-track="t1">6</button>. That is the <dfn class="term" data-def="The fifth note or chord of a key &mdash; it wants to resolve home, not finish.">dominant</dfn> of the opening key of B flat major, which is why the ending feels unfinished.</p><p data-narration-id="q6">The hard rock section shifts into 12/8<button type="button" class="sv-ap-ref" data-t="247" data-track="t1">5</button>, a <dfn class="term" data-def="A metre where each beat divides into three, giving a rolling feel.">compound time</dfn> signature. The rest is almost all 4/4 &mdash; except single bars of 5/4 and 9/8 in the introduction<button type="button" class="sv-ap-ref" data-t="0" data-track="t1">1</button>.</p></div></section><section class="sv-card" data-title="Voices" data-track="t1"><h2>Bohemian Rhapsody: the voices</h2><div class="sv-card-body"><p data-narration-id="q7">Mercury had a four-octave range and uses all of it: soaring falsetto in some places, belted chest voice in others. The word-setting is almost entirely <dfn class="term" data-def="One note for each syllable of text.">syllabic</dfn>. Listen for <dfn class="term" data-def="A smooth slide between two pitches.">portamento</dfn> slides on words like &lsquo;reality&rsquo;<button type="button" class="sv-ap-ref" data-t="0" data-track="t1">1</button>, a <dfn class="term" data-def="A short accented note on the beat followed by a longer note off it.">Scotch snap</dfn> on &lsquo;sympathy&rsquo;, and <dfn class="term" data-def="The music acting out the meaning of the words.">word painting</dfn> as the melody rises at the line about looking up.</p><p data-narration-id="q8">In the opera section<button type="button" class="sv-ap-ref" data-t="183" data-track="t1">4</button> the &lsquo;Galileo&rsquo; motif uses repeated notes and a rising semitone. &lsquo;Bismillah!&rsquo; works as question and answer: the refusal line lands in <dfn class="term" data-def="The same note doubled eight notes apart.">octaves</dfn>, and the group replies in <dfn class="term" data-def="All parts moving together in chords.">homophonic</dfn> chords.</p></div></section><section class="sv-card" data-title="The studio" data-track="t1"><h2>Bohemian Rhapsody: built in the studio</h2><div class="sv-card-body"><div class="key-fact" data-narration-id="q8" data-revision-tip="Cover the box and recall the recording details (artist, year, album) for Bohemian Rhapsody, then check accuracy."><div class="key-fact-label">Key Fact &mdash; The Recording</div><p><em>Bohemian Rhapsody</em> was built in the studio. It uses roughly 180 vocal <dfn class="term" data-def="Extra parts recorded on top of earlier ones, layer by layer.">overdubs</dfn>, bounced across tape generations. The song could not be performed live as recorded.</p></div><p data-narration-id="q9">Brian May&rsquo;s guitar solo at the end of the ballad is virtuosic, with fast <dfn class="term" data-def="Moving up or down the notes of a scale.">scalic</dfn> passages, slides, bends, vibrato and distortion<button type="button" class="sv-ap-ref" data-t="156" data-track="t1">3</button>. In the opera section the drums play the same rhythms as the voices rather than a steady beat, and the staccato crotchet opening imitates the comic characters of real opera<button type="button" class="sv-ap-ref" data-t="183" data-track="t1">4</button>.</p></div></section><section class="sv-card" data-track="t1" data-title="The studio sound"><h2>Bohemian Rhapsody: the studio sound</h2><div class="sv-card-body"><p data-narration-id="qs30">Production is part of the analysis. There is heavy electric guitar overdubbing. The vocals are panned across the stereo field, most clearly in the opera section<button type="button" class="sv-ap-ref" data-t="183" data-track="t1">4</button>. Dynamics swing from alternating quiet and loud phrases to fortissimo interjections, before the final fall away to piano<button type="button" class="sv-ap-ref" data-t="295" data-track="t1">6</button>.</p></div></section><section class="sv-card" data-title="Seven Seas of Rhye" data-track="t2" data-chapters="t2c1,t2c2,t2c3,t2c4,t2c5"><h2>Seven Seas of Rhye: the first hit</h2><div class="sv-card-body"><p data-narration-id="q10">A hard rock song in D major. Released in February 1974, it was the single that made Queen&rsquo;s name. It opens with a ten-bar piano introduction<button type="button" class="sv-ap-ref" data-t="5" data-track="t2">1</button>: an <dfn class="term" data-def="Chords played one note at a time.">arpeggiated</dfn> riff in octaves, in semiquavers. The verse<button type="button" class="sv-ap-ref" data-t="25" data-track="t2">2</button> hits with <dfn class="term" data-def="Bare two-note guitar chords &mdash; root and fifth, no third.">power chords</dfn> underneath.</p><p data-narration-id="q11">After the bridge<button type="button" class="sv-ap-ref" data-t="63" data-track="t2">3</button> and a guitar solo<button type="button" class="sv-ap-ref" data-t="84" data-track="t2">4</button>, the song dissolves into a music-hall singalong &mdash; &lsquo;I Do Like to Be Beside the Seaside&rsquo;<button type="button" class="sv-ap-ref" data-t="157" data-track="t2">5</button> &mdash; played on a <dfn class="term" data-def="A pocket electronic keyboard played with a stylus.">stylophone</dfn>, a completely different style to end on. There is no chorus anywhere: instead a vocal hook closes each verse.</p></div></section><section class="sv-card" data-title="Rhye: sounds to name" data-track="t2"><h2>Seven Seas of Rhye: sounds to name</h2><div class="sv-card-body"><p data-narration-id="q12">The guitar solo moves to B flat major before returning to D, and the second bridge modulates to G major<button type="button" class="sv-ap-ref" data-t="84" data-track="t2">4</button>. Three more details to name: power chords in the introduction<button type="button" class="sv-ap-ref" data-t="5" data-track="t2">1</button>; <dfn class="term" data-def="Expressively lowered notes &mdash; usually the flattened third or seventh.">blues notes</dfn> colouring the vocal line<button type="button" class="sv-ap-ref" data-t="25" data-track="t2">2</button>; palm-muting in the first bridge<button type="button" class="sv-ap-ref" data-t="63" data-track="t2">3</button>; and a delay effect at the end of the guitar solo<button type="button" class="sv-ap-ref" data-t="84" data-track="t2">4</button>.</p><p data-narration-id="q13">The bridge is the contrast section<button type="button" class="sv-ap-ref" data-t="63" data-track="t2">3</button>. The mode shifts, a new melody breaks the verse pattern, and it ends with a long held vocal cry that launches the guitar solo<button type="button" class="sv-ap-ref" data-t="84" data-track="t2">4</button>.</p></div></section><section class="sv-card" data-title="Love of My Life" data-track="t3" data-chapters="t3c1,t3c2,t3c3"><h2>Love of My Life: the ballad</h2><div class="sv-card-body"><p data-narration-id="q16">The ballad of the three. Home is F major, but the harmony travels constantly &mdash; naming where it goes is where the marks are. The short introduction implies F major, then slips to B flat major in its second phrase<button type="button" class="sv-ap-ref" data-t="0" data-track="t3">1</button>.</p><p data-narration-id="qs20">The two verses start in C major and <dfn class="term" data-def="Change key.">modulate</dfn> to F major<button type="button" class="sv-ap-ref" data-t="23" data-track="t3">2</button>. An instrumental bridge in B flat major then echoes that second phrase of the introduction<button type="button" class="sv-ap-ref" data-t="97" data-track="t3">3</button>.</p></div></section><section class="sv-card" data-title="Life: the journey home" data-track="t3" data-chapters="t3c4,t3c5,t3c6"><h2>Love of My Life: the journey home</h2><div class="sv-card-body"><p data-narration-id="qs21">The harp is played by Brian May, who taught himself the instrument for this song &mdash; an orchestral harp is not a normal rock-band sound. An interlude turns to D minor &mdash; the <dfn class="term" data-def="The minor key sharing a key signature with the major: D minor to F major.">relative minor</dfn> &mdash; passing through G minor on the way<button type="button" class="sv-ap-ref" data-t="113" data-track="t3">4</button>.</p><p data-narration-id="qs22">The guitar solo begins in F major, moves to C major, then B flat major<button type="button" class="sv-ap-ref" data-t="143" data-track="t3">5</button>. The coda starts in D minor and finishes in F major: the song only settles at home in its last bars<button type="button" class="sv-ap-ref" data-t="188" data-track="t3">6</button>.</p></div></section><section class="sv-card" data-title="Life: sonority" data-track="t3"><h2>Love of My Life: sonority and texture</h2><div class="sv-card-body"><p data-narration-id="q17"><dfn class="term" data-def="The particular sound quality of voices and instruments &mdash; timbre.">Sonority</dfn> is the topic to reach for here. Mercury sings in his <dfn class="term" data-def="The higher male voice range.">tenor</dfn> range, with moments of <dfn class="term" data-def="A male voice pushed above its natural range, light and airy.">falsetto</dfn>. Around him sit layered backing vocals, harp and arpeggiated piano.</p><p data-narration-id="q18">If a question asks about texture or timbre, this is the track to choose.</p></div></section><section class="sv-card" data-title="One topic"><h2>Three structures, one exam topic</h2><div class="sv-card-body"><p data-narration-id="q19">The spread is deliberate. One multi-sectional epic that breaks the rules of song form. One compact hard rock single. One ballad.</p><p data-narration-id="q20">Comparisons almost write themselves: no chorus in two of the three; piano-led against guitar-led; studio construction against near-live simplicity. Revise them side by side, not one at a time.</p></div></section><section class="sv-card" data-title="Writing about them"><h2>Writing about the tracks</h2><div class="sv-card-body"><p data-narration-id="q21">Marks come from three things: name the element, give its exact technical term, and say where in the track it happens. &lsquo;The harmony is interesting&rsquo; earns nothing.</p><p data-narration-id="q22">&lsquo;The opera section shifts abruptly to A major, an unrelated key&rsquo; earns &mdash; element, term, location.</p></div></section></div>

--- CONCLUSION_HTML ---
<h3 data-narration-id="n21">Key Takeaways</h3>
<ul>
  <li data-narration-id="n22">Bohemian Rhapsody: six sections, no chorus — a cappella B flat opening, ballad to E flat, abrupt A major opera section, 12/8 rock section, quiet F major finish; 180 vocal overdubs and three weeks of recording.</li>
  <li data-narration-id="n23">Seven Seas of Rhye: hard rock in D major with an octave piano riff, ten-bar intro, no chorus, a solo that modulates to the flattened submediant B flat, and a seaside-song fade-out; the February 1974 single that became Queen's first hit.</li>
  <li data-narration-id="n24">Love of My Life: a travelling tonality around F major, sus-chord patterns, harp arpeggios, tenor-and-falsetto lead and a plagal cadence to close.</li>
  <li data-narration-id="n25">Section B formula every time: name the element, give the term, locate it in the track.</li>
</ul>

--- EXAM_TIP_HTML ---
<p data-narration-id="n20">Location wins the mark. Examiners reward &lsquo;the single 5/4 bar in the introduction&rsquo; over &lsquo;the time signature changes sometimes&rsquo;, and &lsquo;the solo modulates to the flattened submediant, B flat major&rsquo; over &lsquo;it changes key&rsquo;. When you revise, attach every term to its lyric or section &mdash; the lyric is your timestamp.</p>

